# Coding conventions

- Business logic lives in `lib/` or `services/` folders, never inline in
  React components or route handlers. Components and route handlers call
  services; they don't contain decision logic themselves.
- Every exported function has an explicit return type annotation. No
  implicit `any`.
- Use early returns and avoid nesting past two levels deep.
- Name booleans with `is`/`has`/`should` prefixes (`isProcessing`, not
  `processing`).
- Every API route: validate the request body with a zod schema before
  touching the database, return a consistent JSON error shape
  (`{ error: { code, message } }`), and never leak internal error detail
  (stack traces, raw SQL errors, provider error payloads) to the client —
  log the detail server-side, return a generic message to the caller.
- Any database write that isn't naturally idempotent (job processing,
  webhook handling, sync jobs) must check whether it already ran before
  acting, using the unique constraints defined in the schema.
- Write plain, readable code a mid-level engineer could review in one pass.
  Prefer clarity over cleverness — no dense one-line chains that need a
  comment to explain what they do; write the clearer multi-line version
  instead.
- Comment only where intent isn't obvious from the code itself (a
  non-obvious business rule, a workaround for a specific known issue) — not
  comments that just restate what the next line does.
- Match existing file and folder naming exactly before adding new files:
  kebab-case for files, PascalCase for React components, camelCase for
  functions and variables.
